WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.170
A streaming company released

2
00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:04.230
movie ratings with the names

3
00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.070
removed for a public contest.

4
00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.070
Researchers matched some of them to

5
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.210
public reviews and re-identified users.

6
00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.300
Removing names did not make

7
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.220
the data anonymous.

8
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.740
Today: how attackers learn whether a

9
00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:22.640
person was in a model's training data,

10
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.340
why removing names is not enough, and

11
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.440
how differential privacy gives a

12
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.540
guarantee students can calculate.

13
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.280
In a membership inference attack, the

14
00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.680
attacker asks whether one person's

15
00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.500
record was in the training data.

16
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.620
Models are often more confident on

17
00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.860
examples they trained on, and that

18
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.280
gap leaks membership.

19
00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:49.050
The bigger the gap between training and

20
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.730
test accuracy, the easier the attack.

21
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.800
A model that memorises its training set

22
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.660
behaves differently on members.

23
00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:59.910
Regularisation helps, but it

24
00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:00.770
gives no guarantee.

25
00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.670
Differential privacy adds calibrated

26
00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:07.550
noise so that any one person changes

27
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.370
the output distribution by at most a

28
00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:11.490
factor set by epsilon.

29
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.340
For a count, one person changes it by one.

30
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.770
The Laplace noise scale is

31
00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.630
sensitivity over epsilon, so

32
00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:23.370
epsilon one half gives scale two.

33
00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.530
To train with differential privacy,

34
00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.890
clip each example's gradient and add

35
00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:32.890
noise before every update, then track

36
00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.330
the total privacy budget.

37
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.280
This bounds membership leakage for

38
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.000
everyone, at some cost in accuracy.

39
00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.000
Students think privacy

40
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.980
means deleting names.

41
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.480
Have them attack a model they trained,

42
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:52.420
measure their success, then retrain

43
00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:56.280
with noise at several epsilons and plot

44
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.640
privacy against accuracy.

45
00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:00.550
Quick check!

46
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.600
A counting query has sensitivity one.

47
00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.980
With epsilon one half, what is

48
00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.720
the Laplace noise scale?

49
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:11.160
Two.

50
00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.870
Sensitivity over epsilon: one

51
00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:16.410
divided by one half.

52
00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:19.780
In the app, open the leak lab.

53
00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:22.820
Guess which records were in training from

54
00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.520
the model's confidence, then add noise

55
00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.860
and see your guesses get worse.

56
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:29.990
Your turn.

57
00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.110
Which step gives a provable

58
00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:35.450
limit on membership leakage for

59
00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:37.590
every person in the data?

60
00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:40.750
Differentially private training.

61
00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.150
Remember: models leak membership through

62
00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.870
confidence, removing names is not

63
00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:49.970
anonymity, and differential privacy trades

64
00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.390
a little accuracy for a calculable

65
00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:52.910
guarantee.

66
00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:56.690
Try it in your next lecture: ask the

67
00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:59.170
class a sensitive yes or no question

68
00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:00.650
by randomised response.

69
00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:03.570
Each student flips a coin first.

70
00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.170
Then estimate the true rate

71
00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.370
together and discuss why no

72
00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.450
single answer reveals anyone.
